    explainerNervousSystemTitle: 'What is the autonomic nervous system?',
    explainerNervousSystem:
      "Without you ever thinking about it, your body constantly manages many things at once: heartbeat, breathing, how your stomach feels. That's the job of the autonomic nervous system — a part of your nervous system working in the background around the clock. It also reacts to what's happening around you, long before you consciously think about it.",
    explainerNeuroceptionTitle: 'How does my body know if it\'s safe?',
    explainerNeuroception:
      'Your nervous system constantly checks — completely automatically — whether a situation feels safe, tense, or threatening. This is called neuroception. A tone of voice, a look, a memory held in the body can be enough. That\'s why something can feel "off" before you can say why — your body has already reacted.',
    explainerNotChoiceTitle: "Why isn't this a conscious choice?",
    explainerNotChoice:
      "Because this assessment happens unconsciously, shifting states usually isn't a decision you make. A reaction is therefore not a personal failure — more an attempt by your body to deal with whatever is happening right now.",
    explainerWhatTitle: 'What does the curve show?',
    explainerWhat:
      "Each check-in is a point on the curve — a short, honest snapshot of how your nervous system feels right now: more calm and connected, more activated, or more withdrawn. Strung together over a day, these points form a picture of your course, not a judgment.",
    explainerColorsTitle: 'What do the three states mean?',
    explainerColors:
      'All three states are normal, useful reactions from your body — none of them is "wrong" or a sign something is off. Calm-and-connected, activated-and-tense, and withdrawn-and-shut-down are simply different ways your nervous system currently deals with the world. The Nervous System page has more on this, including a fuller explanation of each individual state.',
    explainerCoRegulationTitle: 'Why can being near someone be calming?',
    explainerCoRegulation:
      'Nervous systems don\'t just work in isolation — they respond to each other too. A familiar person, a calm voice, or a reassuring look can signal to your system: things are okay here right now. This is called co-regulation, and it\'s one reason closeness can sometimes calm you faster than any thought.',
    explainerNotGoodBadTitle: 'Do I always have to be "connected"?',
    explainerNotGoodBad:
      "No. No state is better or worse than another — each one served a purpose at some point. The point isn't to always be in one particular state, but to more easily recognize and make sense of your own states.",
    explainerSwingsTitle: "Why isn't a swing automatically bad?",
    explainerSwings:
      "Moving between states is exactly what a nervous system does all day long — much like mood or energy naturally shift over a day. Patterns only really become visible over several days: particular times or situations that shift your state. That can help you understand yourself better, not judge yourself.",
    explainerFrameworkTitle: 'Where does this idea come from?',
    explainerFramework:
      "This idea is loosely inspired by Polyvagal Theory (including Deb Dana's work), explained here simply and without jargon. It's a helpful image, not a settled, final explanation — the app doesn't use it as a diagnosis, but as one possible way among several to recognize your own states more easily.",
    explainerPracticalTitle: 'How do I use this day to day?',
    explainerPractical:
      "Once you notice which state you're actually in, you can choose more deliberately what genuinely helps right now — something calming for tension, something gently activating for withdrawal. Without that noticing, you often reach for something more at random. A quick check-in takes seconds, but spread across a day it builds an honest picture — not to judge yourself, but to understand yourself better.",
